---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Lands & Nations · Meridian"
  description="A guide for the Lands plugin on Meridian: claiming, banks, upkeep, tiers, nations, and vassals."
  path="/lands"
  analytics
>
<main class="doc">
  <h1>Lands &amp; Nations</h1>
  <p class="lead">A guide for the Lands plugin. It covers how you claim land, how land banks and upkeep work, how lands unlock tiers, how they combine into nations, and what happens to a nation that loses a war of vassalage.</p>

  <div class="toc">
    <strong>Sections</strong>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#lands">Lands</a></li>
      <li><a href="#nations">Nations</a></li>
      <li><a href="#vassals">Vassals</a></li>
      <li><a href="#registry">Nation registry</a></li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <h2 id="lands"><span class="num">1</span>Lands</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Run <code>/lands create &lt;name&gt;</code> to found your land free of charge. The first chunk is free.</li>
    <li>The next chunk costs <strong>$15,000</strong>, and every chunk after that rises by a fixed <strong>$3,000</strong>, so the larger your land gets, the more each expansion costs.</li>
    <li>When naming your land you can use a hex code to set its color on the world map, for example <code>&lt;#FF5555&gt;ExampleLand</code>. Minecraft only recognizes a specific set of hex colors.</li>
    <li>Your land has its own shared bank, separate from any individual's personal balance. Claim costs and upkeep are withdrawn from this bank, and any member can deposit into it.</li>
    <li>If the bank can't cover a cost, it falls back to the <strong>owner's</strong> personal balance.</li>
    <li>Every land pays weekly upkeep on its claimed chunks, at <strong>$1,500 per chunk</strong>.</li>
    <li>Larger lands cost more to maintain. Before you claim aggressively, make sure your land has the income to support it.</li>
    <li>You can rent or sell sub-areas of your land to other players without adding them as trusted members.</li>
    <li>Lands unlock new tiers by hitting member and wealth thresholds. The tiers are Settlement, Village, Town, Province, and Land. Each tier grants more claimable chunks and member slots, for a modest increase in upkeep.</li>
    <li>You can own only one land at a time, and be a trusted member in one additional land beyond that.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="nations"><span class="num">2</span>Nations</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Multiple lands can form a nation for <strong>$3,500,000</strong>.</li>
    <li>When a land joins a nation it stops paying upkeep to the server and instead pays tax directly to the nation. The nation's leader sets that rate, up to <strong>$6,000 per chunk</strong>.</li>
    <li>In turn, the nation owes its own upkeep to the server, at <strong>$4,000 per chunk</strong> across the combined total of its lands.</li>
    <li>Relations between nations are publicly tracked in-game.</li>
    <li>Like lands, nations can develop into higher tiers. The nation tiers are Federation, Nation, and Empire, and each one unlocks additional claiming capacity for every land in the nation.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="vassals"><span class="num">3</span>Vassals</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>A nation that loses a war of vassalage is absorbed by the winning nation as restricted members.</li>
    <li>Vassals pay taxes to their overlord that can run higher than normal nation taxes.</li>
    <li>The only way to become independent again is to declare and win a war against your overlord. See <a href="/war#goals">Warfare &sect;8</a>.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout">Warfare is the only time claimed land can be entered or captured by an enemy. Read the <a href="/war">Warfare</a> document before you get into a conflict.</div>

  <!-- ================= Nation registry ================= -->
  <section class="analytics" data-analytics>
    <h2 id="registry">Nation registry</h2>
    <p class="as-sub">Every nation on Meridian, ranked by the Meridian Development Index, rebuilt from the land databases.</p>
    <div class="data-stamp" data-stamp>Loading live data&hellip;</div>

    <h3>Nations</h3>
    <div class="nation-grid" data-slot="nation-grid"></div>

    <h3>Land atlas</h3>
    <div data-slot="land-table"></div>

    <details class="method">
      <summary>How the Meridian Development Index is calculated</summary>
      <div class="m-body">
        <p>The MDI is a single score from <code>0</code> to <code>1</code>, modelled on the way the UN builds its Human Development Index: measure a few things that matter, put each on the same scale, then combine them so a nation cannot coast on one strength alone.</p>
        <dl>
          <dt>Territory</dt><dd>Claimed chunks, against a reference ceiling of 60.</dd>
          <dt>Population</dt><dd>Trusted members across the nation's lands, against a ceiling of 15.</dd>
          <dt>Wealth</dt><dd>Combined land-bank treasury, against a ceiling of $5,000,000.</dd>
          <dt>Activity</dt><dd>Recent presence and events in the nation's lands.</dd>
        </dl>
        <p>The four sub-indices are combined with a <strong>geometric mean</strong>, so a nation that is large but empty, or rich but tiny, scores lower than one that is balanced. Reference ceilings are tuned as the server grows.</p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>
</main>
</Base>
